    {
      "id": "snapshot",
      "heading": "Fixed at offer, answered only for the batch",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "md": "The central choice is when a node's context is fixed. Reading the live tree on each query would need no new state, but a re-issued spawn could then be told something different; the work item asked for a snapshot taken at offer time and answered only while its batch is pending. `PendingBatch` holds it as a private field, so `replace` and `acknowledge` keep it in step with the spawns, and the ledger stores only what a spawn ref cannot derive. The query derives `lineage`, `level`, `cap`, `id` and `path` from the ref, and the budget from `node_search_budget`, the one rule the plan entry also uses, so the plan and the query cannot disagree about a budget."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "md": "The cost is a second source of node context beside the tree. The plan notes that level gating already keeps a pending node's known questions stable, so in the ordinary case the snapshot guards re-issues, and it serves a stale question after a mid-batch parent rewrite, which the plan accepts. Decoding it leniently protects the run's claims, but the loss is silent where it happens (`P-snapshot-01`)."
        },
        {
          "kind": "aside",
          "refs": [
            "d-snapshot-answer-from-snapshot",
            "d-snapshot-owned-by-batch",
            "d-snapshot-underivable-only",
            "d-node-query-current-batch-only",
            "d-node-query-budget-in-domain"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "templates",
      "heading": "Templates by command, except the composer's",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "md": "Templates take the same route for a different reason. A path plus a `Read` grant was rejected because the launcher resolves the plugin root through symlinks, so a grant written against `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}` might not match, and an override outside the project would need its own rule (work item, Technical Notes). Having the CLI print the template keeps the plugin root the launcher's concern. Moving the three-tier lookup into `config::template` lets `outstanding --start` name a fallback without a second copy of it. The composer, which reads every note of a pair and so is the agent most exposed to injected content, stays command-free and is given the finding template inline, once per pair."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "md": "The orchestrator's template check runs both commands before the first plan, so a broken or denied template costs no subagents. Its denial branch is the PR's one unobserved outcome, and the unticked manual check may never reach it (`P-templates-01`). Two sources of one template have a side effect: in a run, composers fill the block captured at the check while `single_pass` researchers fetch theirs as they start (`D-03`)."
        },
        {
          "kind": "aside",
          "refs": [
            "d-templates-by-command",
            "d-templates-composer-inline",
            "d-templates-preflight",
            "d-templates-config-crate",
            "d-templates-fallback-on-plan"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "trust",
      "heading": "The guard bounds every reference",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "md": "Passing context by reference widens what a confined researcher may run, so the guard is where this design is safe or not. Each role now has an ordered list of permitted commands, and the researcher's whole surface is four entries: the fetch and the node query as prefixes whose arguments the lexer must clear, and the two template commands matched exactly. The node query's prefix keeps its trailing space, so `end-run` and `outstanding --spawned`, which would let a researcher end or advance the run, stay blocked."
        },
        {
          "kind": "code",
          "file": "cli/research/src/confinement.rs",
          "side": "new",
